var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"kernelmatrix/#kernelmatrix-section","page":"Kernel matrices","text":"While in the introduction we presented a somewhat general way to assemble an HMatrix, abstract matrices associated with an underlying kernel function are common enough in boundary integral equations that a special interface exists for facilitating their use.\n\nThe AbstractKernelMatrix interface is used to represent matrices K with i,j entry given by f(X[i],Y[j]), where X=rowelements(K) and Y=colelements(K). The row and columns elements may be as simple as points in mathbbR^d (as is the case for Nyström methods), but they can also be more complex objects such as triangles or basis functions. In such cases, it is required that center(X[i]) and center(Y[j]) return a point as an SVector, and that f(X[i],Y[j]) make sense.\n\nA concrete implementation of AbstractKernelMatrix is provided by the KernelMatrix type. Creating the matrix associated with the Helmholtz free-space Greens function, for example, can be accomplished through:\n\nusing HMatrices, LinearAlgebra, StaticArrays\nconst Point3D = SVector{3,Float64}\nX = rand(Point3D,10_000)\nY = rand(Point3D,10_000)\nconst k = 2π\nfunction G(x,y)\n    d = norm(x-y)\n    exp(im*k*d)/(4π*d)\nend\nK = KernelMatrix(G,X,Y)\n10000×10000 KernelMatrix{typeof(Main.var\"Main\".G), Vector{StaticArraysCore.SVector{3, Float64}}, Vector{StaticArraysCore.SVector{3, Float64}}, ComplexF64}:\n -0.0453107-0.106765im      -0.110609-0.0751819im   …   -0.168167+0.0662665im\n  -0.111832-0.0741135im     0.0372654-0.0906286im       0.0791568-0.0289281im\n  0.0676824-0.0590757im      0.078971+0.00336069im      -0.168207+0.055641im\n  -0.011236-0.107909im     -0.0594146-0.103454im        0.0547437-0.0760409im\n  0.0789261+0.00358147im   -0.0412307-0.10741im         0.0273735+0.0614285im\n  -0.139238+0.173598im      0.0698619-0.0552364im   …   -0.104981+0.226147im\n  -0.158897-0.000804682im   -0.107067-0.0781464im       -0.139529-0.0418062im\n  -0.139419-0.0419757im     0.0782592+0.00655391im     -0.0306436-0.108443im\n  -0.163266+0.106986im      -0.168243+0.0622898im       -0.069906-0.0998659im\n  0.0462629-0.0839558im     -0.148078-0.0269882im       0.0568523-0.0737583im\n           ⋮                                        ⋱  \n  0.0261996-0.0970027im     -0.114263-0.0719186im      -0.0604536-0.103142im\n  0.0594282-0.0707599im     -0.118276-0.068076im       -0.0978604-0.0850243im\n  -0.154278-0.0134622im      -0.16809+0.068532im        0.0220981-0.0989382im\n  0.0592928-0.0709237im    -0.0840872-0.0933689im       0.0461978-0.0840098im\n   0.026941-0.0966298im      0.330132+0.432549im    …   0.0568319-0.0737811im\n -0.0972792-0.0854213im    -0.0558381+0.276954im        -0.102151-0.0819603im\n  0.0748691-0.0442438im     0.0163102-0.101323im        0.0688802-0.0570179im\n -0.0795635-0.095658im      -0.107801-0.0775474im       0.0530888-0.0777354im\n  -0.166649+0.0865839im     -0.107644-0.0776759im       0.0119131-0.102882im\n\nCompressing K is now as simple as:\n\nH = assemble_hmatrix(K;rtol=1e-6)\nHMatrix of ComplexF64 with range 1:10000 × 1:10000\n\t number of nodes in tree: 14681\n\t number of leaves: 11011 (2776 admissible + 8235 full)\n\t min rank of sparse blocks : 20\n\t max rank of sparse blocks : 86\n\t min length of dense blocks : 450\n\t max length of dense blocks : 2850\n\t min number of elements per leaf: 450\n\t max number of elements per leaf: 445550\n\t depth of tree: 9\n\t compression ratio: 2.465165\n\n\nThe elements are clustered by their center, and the keyword arguments are those of assemble_hmatrix. If the entries of the kernel need more than the elements (e.g. per-element data), any matrix K indexed by (i, j) can be assembled together with its points, as assemble_hmatrix(K, X, Y); see the Assembling an HMatrix section.\n\nAs before, you can multiply H by a vector, or do an lu factorization of it.\n\nFinally, here is a somewhat contrived example of how to use a KernelMatrix when the rowelements and colelements are not simply points (as required e.g. in a Galerkin discretization of boundary integral equations):\n\nusing HMatrices, LinearAlgebra, StaticArrays\n# create a simple structure to represent a segment\nstruct Segment\n    start::SVector{2,Float64}\n    stop::SVector{2,Float64}\nend\n# extend the function center to work with segments\nHMatrices.center(s::Segment) = 0.5*(s.start + s.stop)\n# P1 mesh of a circle\nnpts = 10_000\nnodes = [SVector(cos(s), sin(s)) for s in range(0,stop=2π,length=npts+1)]\nsegments = [Segment(nodes[i],nodes[i+1]) for i in 1:npts]\n# Now define a kernel function that takes two segments (instead of two points) and returns a scalar\nfunction G(target::Segment,source::Segment)\n    x, y = HMatrices.center(target), HMatrices.center(source)\n    d = norm(x-y)\n    return -log(d + 1e-10)\nend\nK = KernelMatrix(G,segments,segments)\n# compress the kernel matrix\nH = assemble_hmatrix(K;rtol=1e-6)\nHMatrix of Float64 with range 1:10000 × 1:10000\n\t number of nodes in tree: 3341\n\t number of leaves: 2506 (798 admissible + 1708 full)\n\t min rank of sparse blocks : 4\n\t max rank of sparse blocks : 7\n\t min length of dense blocks : 625\n\t max length of dense blocks : 4300\n\t min number of elements per leaf: 625\n\t max number of elements per leaf: 2775556\n\t depth of tree: 9\n\t compression ratio: 23.325884\n","title":"Kernel matrices"},{"category":"section","location":"kernelmatrix/#Support-for-tensor-kernels","page":"Kernel matrices","text":"warning: Warning\nSupport for tensor-valued kernels should be considered experimental at this stage.\n\nFor vector-valued partial differential equations such as Stokes or time-harmonic Maxwell's equation, the underlying integral operator has a kernel function which is a tensor. This package currently provides some limited support for these types of operators. The example below illustrates how to build an HMatrix representing a KernelMatrix corresponding to Stokes Greens function for points on a sphere:\n\nusing HMatrices, LinearAlgebra, StaticArrays\nconst Point3D = SVector{3,Float64}\nm = 5_000\nX = Y = [Point3D(sin(θ)cos(ϕ),sin(θ)*sin(ϕ),cos(θ)) for (θ,ϕ) in zip(π*rand(m),2π*rand(m))]\nconst μ = 5\nfunction G(x,y)\n    r = x-y\n    d = norm(r) + 1e-10\n    1/(8π*μ) * (1/d*I + r*transpose(r)/d^3)\nend\nK = KernelMatrix(G,X,Y)\nH = assemble_hmatrix(K;atol=1e-4)\nHMatrix of StaticArraysCore.SMatrix{3, 3, Float64, 9} with range 1:5000 × 1:5000\n\t number of nodes in tree: 4005\n\t number of leaves: 3004 (812 admissible + 2192 full)\n\t min rank of sparse blocks : 7\n\t max rank of sparse blocks : 28\n\t min length of dense blocks : 144\n\t max length of dense blocks : 11368\n\t min number of elements per leaf: 144\n\t max number of elements per leaf: 103632\n\t depth of tree: 10\n\t compression ratio: 3.308898\n\n\nYou can now multiply H by a density σ, where σ is a Vector of SVector{3,Float64}\n\nσ = rand(SVector{3,Float64},m)\ny = H*σ\n# test the output agains the exact value for a given `i`\ni = 42\nexact = sum(K[i,j]*σ[j] for j in 1:m)\n@show y[i] - exact\n3-element StaticArraysCore.SVector{3, Float64} with indices SOneTo(3):\n 4.3138861656188965e-6\n 8.121132850646973e-6\n 1.1026859283447266e-6\n\nnote: Note\nThe naive idea of reinterpreting these matrices of tensors as a (larger) matrix of scalars does not always work because care to be taken when choosing the pivot in the compression stage of the PartialACA in order to exploit some analytic properties of the underlying kernel. See e.g. section 2.3 of this paper for a brief discussion.","title":"Support for tensor kernels"},{"category":"section","location":"references/#references-section","page":"References","text":"","title":"References"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#Base.Matrix-Tuple{Union{HMatrix, LinearAlgebra.Adjoint{T, HMatrix{R, T}} where {R, T}, LinearAlgebra.Hermitian{T, HMatrix{R, T}} where {R, T}}}","page":"References","text":"Matrix(H::HMatrix)\n\nConvert H to a dense Matrix, in the ordering of the points used to assemble it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.Matrix"},{"category":"type","location":"references/#HMatrices.ACAWithRecompression","page":"References","text":"struct ACAWithRecompression\n\nA two-stage compressor that applies PartialACA followed by recompression via TSVD. The ACA step produces a low-rank approximation quickly using partial pivoting; the subsequent SVD step can reduce the rank further, at the cost of a small additional computation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.ACAWithRecompression"},{"category":"type","location":"references/#HMatrices.AbstractCompressor","page":"References","text":"abstract type AbstractCompressor\n\nTypes used to compress matrices. A compressor comp is called as comp(K, irange, jrange, buf) and returns an RkMatrix approximating K[irange, jrange], where buf is a workspace created by allocate_buffer(comp, T) (nothing by default). During the assembly of an HMatrix, it is called with the index ranges of each admissible block.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.AbstractCompressor"},{"category":"type","location":"references/#HMatrices.AbstractKernelMatrix","page":"References","text":"abstract type AbstractKernelMatrix{T} <: AbstractMatrix{T}\n\nInterface for abstract matrices represented through a kernel function f, target elements X, and source elements Y. The matrix entry i,j is given by f(X[i],Y[j]). Concrete subtypes should implement Base.size, Base.getindex(K, i::Int, j::Int), and rowelements and colelements, whose elements have a center used to cluster them.\n\nImplementing getblock! (e.g. with SIMD vectorization) can improve the speed of assembling an HMatrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.AbstractKernelMatrix"},{"category":"type","location":"references/#HMatrices.AbstractSplitter","page":"References","text":"abstract type AbstractSplitter\n\nAn AbstractSplitter is used to split a ClusterTree. The interface requires the following methods:\n\nshould_split(clt,depth,splitter) : return a Bool determining if the ClusterTree should be further divided\nsplit!(clt,splitter) : perform the splitting of the ClusterTree handling the necessary data sorting.\n\nSee GeometricSplitter for an example of an implementation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.AbstractSplitter"},{"category":"type","location":"references/#HMatrices.CardinalitySplitter","page":"References","text":"struct CardinalitySplitter <: AbstractSplitter\n\nUsed to split a ClusterTree along the largest dimension if length(tree)>nmax. The split is performed so the data is evenly distributed amongst all children.\n\nSee also: AbstractSplitter\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.CardinalitySplitter"},{"category":"type","location":"references/#HMatrices.ClusterTree","page":"References","text":"ClusterTree(elements,splitter;[copy_elements=true, threads=false])\n\nConstruct a ClusterTree from the  given elements using the splitting strategy encoded in splitter. If copy_elements is set to false, the elements argument are directly stored in the ClusterTree and are permuted during the tree construction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.ClusterTree"},{"category":"type","location":"references/#HMatrices.ClusterTree-2","page":"References","text":"mutable struct ClusterTree{N,T}\n\nTree structure used to cluster points of type SVector{N,T} into HyperRectangles.\n\nFields:\n\n_elements::Vector{SVector{N,T}} : vector containing the sorted elements.\ncontainer::HyperRectangle{N,T} : container for the elements in the current node.\nindex_range::UnitRange{Int} : indices of elements contained in the current node.\nloc2glob::Vector{Int} : permutation from the local indexing system to the original (global) indexing system used as input in the construction of the tree.\nglob2loc::Vector{Int} : inverse of loc2glob permutation.\nchildren::Vector{ClusterTree{N,T}}\nparentnode::ClusterTree{N,T}\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.ClusterTree"},{"category":"type","location":"references/#HMatrices.GeometricSplitter","page":"References","text":"struct GeometricSplitter <: AbstractSplitter\n\nUsed to split a ClusterTree in half along the largest axis. The children boxes are shrunk to tightly fit the data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.GeometricSplitter"},{"category":"type","location":"references/#HMatrices.HMatrix","page":"References","text":"mutable struct HMatrix{R,T} <: AbstractMatrix{T}\n\nA hierarchical matrix constructed from a rowtree and coltree of type R and holding elements of type T.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.HMatrix"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices.HMatrix-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{R}, Tuple{R, R, Any}} where {R, T}","page":"References","text":"HMatrix{T}(rowtree,coltree,adm)\n\nConstruct an empty HMatrix with rowtree and coltree using the admissibility condition adm. This function builds the skeleton for the hierarchical matrix, but does not compute data field in the blocks. See assemble_hmatrix for assembling a hierarchical matrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.HMatrix"},{"category":"type","location":"references/#HMatrices.HyperRectangle","page":"References","text":"struct HyperRectangle{N,T}\n\nAxis-aligned hyperrectangle in N dimensions given by low_corner::SVector{N,T} and high_corner::SVector{N,T}.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.HyperRectangle"},{"category":"type","location":"references/#HMatrices.KernelMatrix","page":"References","text":"KernelMatrix{Tf,Tx,Ty,T} <:: AbstractKernelMatrix{T}\n\nGeneric kernel matrix representing a kernel function acting on two sets of elements. If K is a KernelMatrix, then K[i,j] = f(X[i],Y[j]) where f::Tf=kernel(K), X::Tx=rowelements(K) and Y::Ty=colelements(K).\n\nExamples\n\nX = rand(SVector{2,Float64},2)\nY = rand(SVector{2,Float64},2)\nK = KernelMatrix(X,Y) do x,y\n    sum(x+y)\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.KernelMatrix"},{"category":"type","location":"references/#HMatrices.MulLinearOp","page":"References","text":"struct MulLinearOp{T,V,S} <: AbstractMatrix{T}\n\nAbstract matrix representing the following linear operator:\n\n    L = R + P + a * ∑ᵢ Aᵢ * Bᵢ\n\nwhere R and P are of type RkMatrix{T}, Aᵢ,Bᵢ are of type HMatrix{R,T} and a is scalar multiplier. Calling compressor(L) produces a low-rank approximation of L, where compressor is an AbstractCompressor.\n\nNote: this structure is used to group the operations required when multiplying hierarchical matrices so that they can later be executed in a way that minimizes recompression of intermediate computations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.MulLinearOp"},{"category":"type","location":"references/#HMatrices.PartialACA","page":"References","text":"struct PartialACA\n\nAdaptive cross approximation algorithm with partial pivoting. This structure can be used to generate an RkMatrix from a matrix-like object M as follows:\n\nusing LinearAlgebra\nusing HMatrices: PartialACA\nrtol = 1e-6\ncomp = PartialACA(;rtol)\nA = rand(10,2)\nB = rand(10,2)\nM = A*adjoint(B) # a low-rank matrix\nR = comp(M, axes(M)...) # compress the entire matrix `M`\nnorm(Matrix(R) - M) < rtol*norm(M) # true\n\n# output\n\ntrue\n\n\nBecause it uses partial pivoting, the linear operator does not have to be evaluated at every i,j. This is usually much faster than TSVD, but due to the pivoting strategy the algorithm may fail in special cases, even when the underlying linear operator is of low rank.\n\nThe default rtol is sqrt(eps(R)), where R is the real type underlying the entries of the compressed matrix, or only the accuracy attainable with R if atol or rank is given. An rtol smaller than that accuracy (10eps(R)) is raised to it, or throws an ArgumentError if it is positive, so the rank of a block can stay below rank when the rest of the block is at the level of rounding errors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.PartialACA"},{"category":"type","location":"references/#HMatrices.PermutedMatrix","page":"References","text":"PermutedMatrix{K,T} <: AbstractMatrix{T}\n\nStructure used to represent the permutation of a matrix-like object. The original matrix is stored in the data::K field, and the permutations are stored in rowperm and colperm.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.PermutedMatrix"},{"category":"type","location":"references/#HMatrices.PrincipalComponentSplitter","page":"References","text":"struct PrincipalComponentSplitter <: AbstractSplitter\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.PrincipalComponentSplitter"},{"category":"type","location":"references/#HMatrices.RkMatrix","page":"References","text":"mutable struct RkMatrix{T}\n\nRepresentation of a rank r matrix M in outer product format M = A*adjoint(B) where A has size m × r and B has size n × r.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.RkMatrix"},{"category":"type","location":"references/#HMatrices.StrongAdmissibilityStd","page":"References","text":"struct StrongAdmissibilityStd\n\nTwo blocks are admissible under this condition if the minimum of their diameter is smaller than eta times the distance between them, where eta::Float64 is a parameter.\n\nUsage:\n\nadm = StrongAdmissibilityStd(;eta=2.0)\nadm(Xnode,Ynode)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.StrongAdmissibilityStd"},{"category":"type","location":"references/#HMatrices.TSVD","page":"References","text":"struct TSVD\n\nCompression algorithm based on a posteriori truncation of an SVD. This is the optimal approximation in Frobenius norm; however, it also tends to be very expensive and thus should be used mostly for \"small\" matrices. The default rtol is the same as for PartialACA.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.TSVD"},{"category":"type","location":"references/#HMatrices.VectorOfVectors","page":"References","text":"struct VectorOfVectors{T}\n\nA simple structure which behaves as a Vector{Vector{T}} but stores the entries in a contiguous data::Vector{T} field. All vectors in the VectorOfVectors are assumed to be of size m, and there are k of them, meaning this structure can be used to represent a m × k matrix.\n\nSimilar to a vector-of-vectors, calling A[i] returns a view to the i-th column.\n\nSee also: newcol!\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.VectorOfVectors"},{"category":"type","location":"references/#HMatrices.WeakAdmissibilityStd","page":"References","text":"struct WeakAdmissibilityStd\n\nTwo blocks are admissible under this condition if the distance between them is positive.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.WeakAdmissibilityStd"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#Base.split-Union{Tuple{N}, Tuple{HMatrices.HyperRectangle{N}, Any, Any}} where N","page":"References","text":"split(rec::HyperRectangle,[axis]::Int,[place])\n\nSplit a hyperrectangle in two along the axis direction at the  position place. Returns a tuple with the two resulting hyperrectangles.\n\nWhen no place is given, defaults to splitting in the middle of the axis.\n\nWhen no axis and no place is given, defaults to splitting along the largest axis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.split"},{"category":"function","location":"references/#HMatrices._aca_partial","page":"References","text":"_aca_partial(K,irange,jrange,atol,rmax,rtol,istart=1)\n\nInternal function implementing the adaptive cross-approximation algorithm with partial pivoting. The returned R::RkMatrix provides an approximation to M = K[irange,jrange] of rank at most rmax, which is expected to satisfy |M - R| < max(atol,rtol*|M|), but this inequality may fail to hold due to the various errors involved in estimating the error and |M|.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices._aca_partial"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices._aca_partial_pivot-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"References","text":"_aca_partial_pivot(v,I)\n\nFind in the valid set I the index of the element x ∈ v maximizing its smallest singular value. This is equivalent to minimizing the spectral norm of the inverse of x.\n\nWhen x is a scalar, this is simply the element with largest absolute value. Return -1 if I has no valid index.\n\nThis general implementation should work for both scalar as well as tensor-valued kernels; see https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0021999117306721 for more details.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices._aca_partial_pivot"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices._assemble_cpu!-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"References","text":"_assemble_cpu!(hmat::HMatrix,K,comp)\n\nAssemble data on the leaves of hmat. The admissible leaves are compressed using the compressor comp. This function assumes the structure of hmat has already been initialized, and therefore should not be called directly. See HMatrix for information on constructors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices._assemble_cpu!"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices._assemble_threads!-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"References","text":"_assemble_threads!(hmat::HMatrix,K,comp)\n\nLike _assemble_cpu!, but uses threads to assemble the leaves. Note that the threads are spawned using Threads.@spawn, which means they are spawned on the same worker as the caller.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices._assemble_threads!"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices._build_block_structure!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{R}, Tuple{Any, HMatrix{R, T}}} where {R, T}","page":"References","text":"_build_block_structure!(adm_fun,current_node)\n\nRecursive constructor for HMatrix block structure. Should not be called directly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices._build_block_structure!"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices._compress-NTuple{5, Any}","page":"References","text":"_compress(comp, K, rowtree, coltree, buf)\n\nCompress the block of K with rows in rowtree and columns in coltree. Calls comp(K, irange, jrange, buf) with the index ranges of the trees, except for compressors that use the geometry of the clusters.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices._compress"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices._hgemv_recursive!-Tuple{AbstractVector, Union{HMatrix, LinearAlgebra.Adjoint{T, HMatrix{R, T}} where {R, T}, LinearAlgebra.Hermitian{T, HMatrix{R, T}} where {R, T}, LinearAlgebra.LowerTriangular{<:Any, <:Union{HMatrix, LinearAlgebra.Adjoint{T, HMatrix{R, T}} where {R, T}, LinearAlgebra.Hermitian{T, HMatrix{R, T}} where {R, T}}}, LinearAlgebra.UnitLowerTriangular{<:Any, <:Union{HMatrix, LinearAlgebra.Adjoint{T, HMatrix{R, T}} where {R, T}, LinearAlgebra.Hermitian{T, HMatrix{R, T}} where {R, T}}}, LinearAlgebra.UnitUpperTriangular{<:Any, <:Union{HMatrix, LinearAlgebra.Adjoint{T, HMatrix{R, T}} where {R, T}, LinearAlgebra.Hermitian{T, HMatrix{R, T}} where {R, T}}}, LinearAlgebra.UpperTriangular{<:Any, <:Union{HMatrix, LinearAlgebra.Adjoint{T, HMatrix{R, T}} where {R, T}, LinearAlgebra.Hermitian{T, HMatrix{R, T}} where {R, T}}}}, AbstractVector, Any}","page":"References","text":"_hgemv_recursive!(C,A,B,offset)\n\nInternal function used to compute C[I] <-- C[I] + A*B[J] where I = rowrange(A) - offset[1] and J = rowrange(B) - offset[2].\n\nThe offset argument is used on the caller side to signal if the original hierarchical matrix had a pivot other than (1,1).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices._hgemv_recursive!"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices._hmul_tree!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{T, Union{HMatrix, LinearAlgebra.Adjoint{T, HMatrix{R, T}} where {R, T}, LinearAlgebra.Hermitian{T, HMatrix{R, T}} where {R, T}}, Union{HMatrix, LinearAlgebra.Adjoint{T, HMatrix{R, T}} where {R, T}, LinearAlgebra.Hermitian{T, HMatrix{R, T}} where {R, T}}, Any, Any, Any}, Tuple{T, Union{HMatrix, LinearAlgebra.Adjoint{T, HMatrix{R, T}} where {R, T}, LinearAlgebra.Hermitian{T, HMatrix{R, T}} where {R, T}}, Union{HMatrix, LinearAlgebra.Adjoint{T, HMatrix{R, T}} where {R, T}, LinearAlgebra.Hermitian{T, HMatrix{R, T}} where {R, T}}, Vararg{Any, 4}}, Tuple{T, Union{HMatrix, LinearAlgebra.Adjoint{T, HMatrix{R, T}} where {R, T}, LinearAlgebra.Hermitian{T, HMatrix{R, T}} where {R, T}}, Union{HMatrix, LinearAlgebra.Adjoint{T, HMatrix{R, T}} where {R, T}, LinearAlgebra.Hermitian{T, HMatrix{R, T}} where {R, T}}, Vararg{Any, 5}}} where T<:HMatrix","page":"References","text":"_hmul_tree!(C, A, B, a, b, compressor, bufs = nothing, Cflag = 'N')\n\nCompute C <-- A*B*a + C*b in the ordering in which the H-matrices are stored, i.e. as products of the stored blocks, whatever the points they come from.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices._hmul_tree!"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices._mul_tree!-NTuple{5, Any}","page":"References","text":"_mul_tree!(y, A, x, b, threads)\n\nPerform y <-- A*x + y*b in the ordering of the trees of A.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices._mul_tree!"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices._rkfactors-Tuple{HMatrices.RkMatrix}","page":"References","text":"_rkfactors(R)\n\nThe factors (U, V) of a low-rank matrix R = U*adjoint(V).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices._rkfactors"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices._rtol-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"References","text":"_rtol(rtol, atol, rank, T)\n\nThe relative tolerance used to compress a matrix with entries of type T, where R is the real type underlying T. If rtol is nothing, it is sqrt(eps(R)), or the accuracy attainable with R (10eps(R)) if atol or rank is given. Throw an ArgumentError if rtol is below that accuracy.\n\nAn rtol of zero is raised to that accuracy, so that an atol too small for a block does not compress it to full rank.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices._rtol"},{"category":"function","location":"references/#HMatrices._tree_matrix","page":"References","text":"_tree_matrix(H, T = eltype(H))\n\nThe dense Matrix{T} of H in the ordering of its cluster trees, i.e. the ordering in which H is stored.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices._tree_matrix"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices._update_frob_norm-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"References","text":"_update_frob_norm(acc,A,B)\n\nGiven the Frobenius norm of Rₖ = A[1:end-1]*adjoint(B[1:end-1]) in acc, compute the Frobenius norm of Rₖ₊₁ = A*adjoint(B) efficiently.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices._update_frob_norm"},{"category":"function","location":"references/#HMatrices.allocate_buffer","page":"References","text":"allocate_buffer(comp, T)\n\nA workspace for the compressor comp on matrices with entries of type T, which can be passed to (and reused by) its calls; nothing if comp needs none.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.allocate_buffer"},{"category":"function","location":"references/#HMatrices.assemble_hmatrix","page":"References","text":"assemble_hmatrix(K, X, Y = X; atol = 0, rtol, rank, eta = 3, nmax = 50, threads = true)\n\nApproximate the matrix K by an HMatrix, where X and Y are the points (SVectors, or elements with a center) associated with the rows and the columns of K. The points are only used to cluster the indices, and are not modified.\n\nK can be any AbstractMatrix (e.g. a KernelMatrix), with scalar or SMatrix entries; getblock! can be overloaded to compute its blocks faster. If K is a Hermitian, only its upper triangle is assembled and a Hermitian HMatrix is returned (this requires Y === X).\natol, rtol and rank control the compression of the low-rank blocks (see PartialACA); by default rtol = sqrt(eps) of the real type of the entries, or only the accuracy attainable with that type if atol or rank is given.\nTwo clusters are compressed if min(diameters) < eta * distance; eta = Inf compresses every pair of disjoint clusters (weak admissibility).\nnmax is the maximum number of points in a leaf of the cluster trees.\n\nEvery operation on the result (products, Matrix, factorizations) uses the ordering of X and Y.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.assemble_hmatrix"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices.assemble_hmatrix-Tuple{AbstractKernelMatrix}","page":"References","text":"assemble_hmatrix(K::AbstractKernelMatrix; kwargs...)\nassemble_hmatrix(Hermitian(K::AbstractKernelMatrix); kwargs...)\n\nApproximate K by an HMatrix, clustering the elements of K by their center. The keyword arguments are those of assemble_hmatrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.assemble_hmatrix"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices.assemble_hmatrix-Tuple{AbstractMatrix, HMatrices.ClusterTree, HMatrices.ClusterTree}","page":"References","text":"assemble_hmatrix(K, rowtree::ClusterTree, coltree::ClusterTree;\n    adm = StrongAdmissibilityStd(), comp = PartialACA(), threads = true)\n\nLower-level version of assemble_hmatrix with given cluster trees, admissibility condition adm (called on a node of rowtree and a node of coltree to decide if the block is compressed) and compressor comp (see AbstractCompressor).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.assemble_hmatrix"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices.binary_split!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{N}, Tuple{HMatrices.ClusterTree{N, T}, Function}} where {N, T}","page":"References","text":"binary_split!(cluster::ClusterTree,predicate)\n\nSplit a ClusterTree into two, sorting all elements in the process according to predicate. cluster is assigned as parent to each children.\n\nEach point is sorted according to whether f(x) returns true (point sorted on the \"left\" node) or false (point sorted on the \"right\" node). At the end a minimal HyperRectangle containing all left/right points is created.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.binary_split!"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices.center-Tuple{HMatrices.HyperRectangle}","page":"References","text":"center(Ω)\n\nCenter of the smallest ball containing Ω.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.center"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices.colelements-Tuple{KernelMatrix}","page":"References","text":"colelements(K::AbstractKernelMatrix)\n\nThe elements associated with the columns of K.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.colelements"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices.colperm-Tuple{Any}","page":"References","text":"colperm(H)\n\nLike rowperm, for the columns of H.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.colperm"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices.compress!-Tuple{HMatrices.RkMatrix, HMatrices.TSVD}","page":"References","text":"compress!(M::RkMatrix,tsvd::TSVD)\n\nRecompress the matrix R using a truncated svd of R. The implementation uses the qr-svd strategy to efficiently compute svd(R) when rank(R) ≪ min(size(R)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.compress!"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices.compress!-Tuple{Matrix, HMatrices.TSVD}","page":"References","text":"compress!(M::Matrix,tsvd::TSVD)\n\nRecompress the matrix M using a truncated svd and output an RkMatrix. The data in M is invalidated in the process.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.compress!"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices.compression_ratio-Tuple{Union{HMatrix, LinearAlgebra.Adjoint{T, HMatrix{R, T}} where {R, T}, LinearAlgebra.Hermitian{T, HMatrix{R, T}} where {R, T}}}","page":"References","text":"compression_ratio(H::HTypes)\n\nThe ratio of the uncompressed size of H to its compressed size. A compression_ratio of 10 means it would have taken 10 times more memory to store H as a dense matrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.compression_ratio"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices.container-Tuple{HMatrices.ClusterTree}","page":"References","text":"container(clt::ClusterTree)\n\nReturn the object enclosing all the elements of the clt.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.container"},{"category":"function","location":"references/#HMatrices.depth","page":"References","text":"depth(tree,acc=0)\n\nRecursive function to compute the depth of node in a tree-like structure.\n\nOverload this function if your structure has a more efficient way to compute depth (e.g. if it stores it in a field).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.depth"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices.diameter-Tuple{HMatrices.HyperRectangle}","page":"References","text":"diameter(Ω)\n\nLargest distance between x and y for x,y ∈ Ω.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.diameter"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices.distance-Tuple{HMatrices.ClusterTree, HMatrices.ClusterTree}","page":"References","text":"distance(X::ClusterTree, Y::ClusterTree)\n\nDistance between the containers of X and Y.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.distance"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices.distance-Union{Tuple{N}, Tuple{HMatrices.HyperRectangle{N}, HMatrices.HyperRectangle{N}}} where N","page":"References","text":"distance(Ω1,Ω2)\n\nMinimal Euclidean distance between a point x ∈ Ω1 and y ∈ Ω2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.distance"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices.elements-Tuple{HMatrices.ClusterTree}","page":"References","text":"elements(clt::ClusterTree)\n\nIterable list of the elements inside clt.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.elements"},{"category":"function","location":"references/#HMatrices.filter_tree","page":"References","text":"filter_tree(f,tree,isterminal=true)\n\nReturn a vector containing all the nodes of tree such that f(node)==true.  The argument isterminal can be used to control whether to continue the search on children of nodes for which f(node)==true.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.filter_tree"},{"category":"function","location":"references/#HMatrices.filter_tree!","page":"References","text":"filter_tree!(filter,nodes,tree,[isterminal=true])\n\nLike filter_tree, but appends results to nodes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.filter_tree!"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices.getblock!-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"References","text":"getblock!(block,K,irange,jrange)\n\nFill block with K[i,j] for i ∈ irange, j ∈ jrange, where block is of size length(irange) × length(jrange).\n\nA default implementation exists which relies on getindex(K,i,j), but this method can be overloaded for better performance if e.g. a vectorized way of computing a block is available. The indices are in the ordering of K, and irange/jrange can be any vectors of integers, not only ranges.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.getblock!"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices.getcol!-Tuple{Any, Any, Any}","page":"References","text":"getcol!(col, M, j)\n\nReturn the j-th column of M in col.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.getcol!"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices.getcol!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Any, Union{HMatrices.RkMatrix, LinearAlgebra.Adjoint{<:Any, <:HMatrices.RkMatrix}}, Int64}, Tuple{Any, Union{HMatrices.RkMatrix, LinearAlgebra.Adjoint{<:Any, <:HMatrices.RkMatrix}}, Int64, Val{T}}} where T","page":"References","text":"getcol!(col, R, j[, Val(add)])\n\nFill col with column j of the low-rank matrix R, or add it to col if add is true.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.getcol!"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices.getcol-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"References","text":"getcol(M, j)\n\nReturn the j-th column of M.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.getcol"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices.glob2loc-Tuple{HMatrices.ClusterTree}","page":"References","text":"glob2loc(clt::ClusterTree)\n\nThe inverse of loc2glob.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.glob2loc"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices.hmul!-Tuple{Union{HMatrix, LinearAlgebra.Hermitian{T, HMatrix{R, T}} where {R, T}}, Union{HMatrix, LinearAlgebra.Adjoint{T, HMatrix{R, T}} where {R, T}, LinearAlgebra.Hermitian{T, HMatrix{R, T}} where {R, T}}, Union{HMatrix, LinearAlgebra.Adjoint{T, HMatrix{R, T}} where {R, T}, LinearAlgebra.Hermitian{T, HMatrix{R, T}} where {R, T}}, Any, Any, Any}","page":"References","text":"hmul!(C::HMatrix, A::HMatrix, B::HMatrix, a, b, compressor)\n\nSimilar to mul! : compute C <-- A*B*a + C*b, where A,B,C are hierarchical matrices and compressor is a function/functor used in the intermediate stages of the multiplication to avoid growing the rank of admissible blocks after addition is performed.\n\nThe rows of A and C, the columns of A and the rows of B, and the columns of B and C must be clustered in the same way (e.g. built from the same points); otherwise an ArgumentError is thrown.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.hmul!"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices.index_range-Tuple{HMatrices.ClusterTree}","page":"References","text":"index_range(clt::ClusterTree)\n\nIndices of elements in root_elements(clt) which lie inside clt.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.index_range"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices.isclean-Tuple{HMatrix}","page":"References","text":"isclean(H::HMatrix)\n\nReturn true if all leaves of H have data, and if the leaves are the only nodes containing data. This is the normal state of an ℋ-matrix, but during intermediate stages of a computation data may be associated with non-leaf nodes for convenience.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.isclean"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices.leaves-Tuple{Any}","page":"References","text":"leaves(tree)\n\nReturn a vector containing all the leaf nodes of tree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.leaves"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices.loc2glob-Tuple{HMatrices.ClusterTree}","page":"References","text":"loc2glob(clt::ClusterTree)\n\nThe permutation from the (local) indexing system of the elements of the clt to the (global) indexes used upon the construction of the tree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.loc2glob"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices.newcol!-Tuple{HMatrices.VectorOfVectors}","page":"References","text":"newcol!(A::VectorOfVectors)\n\nAppend a new (uninitialized) column to A, and return a view of it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.newcol!"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices.nodes-Tuple{Any}","page":"References","text":"nodes(tree)\n\nReturn a vector containing all the nodes of tree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.nodes"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices.parentnode-Tuple{HMatrices.ClusterTree}","page":"References","text":"parentnode(clt::ClusterTree)\n\nThe node's parent. If t is a root, then parent(t)==t.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.parentnode"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices.radius-Tuple{HMatrices.HyperRectangle}","page":"References","text":"radius(Ω)\n\nHalf the diameter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.radius"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices.reset!-Tuple{HMatrices.VectorOfVectors}","page":"References","text":"reset!(A::VectorOfVectors)\n\nSet the number of columns of A to zero, and the number of rows to zero, but does not resize! the underlying data vector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.reset!"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices.root_elements-Tuple{HMatrices.ClusterTree}","page":"References","text":"root_elements(clt::ClusterTree)\n\nThe elements contained in the root of the tree to which clt belongs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.root_elements"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices.rowelements-Tuple{KernelMatrix}","page":"References","text":"rowelements(K::AbstractKernelMatrix)\n\nThe elements associated with the rows of K.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.rowelements"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#HMatrices.rowperm-Tuple{Any}","page":"References","text":"rowperm(H)\n\nThe permutation of the rows of the whole matrix into the ordering in which it is stored: for the root H of an HMatrix, the stored row i is the row rowperm(H)[i] of the matrix approximated by H. For a block of H, it is the permutation of the whole matrix (the rows of the block are rowperm(H)[rowrange(H)]).\n\nThe returned vector belongs to the cluster tree and must not be modified.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.rowperm"},{"category":"function","location":"references/#HMatrices.should_split","page":"References","text":"should_split(clt::ClusterTree, depth, splitter::AbstractSplitter)\n\nDetermine whether or not a ClusterTree should be further divided.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.should_split"},{"category":"function","location":"references/#HMatrices.split!","page":"References","text":"split!(clt::ClusterTree,splitter::AbstractSplitter)\n\nDivide clt using the strategy implemented by splitter. This function is responsible for assigning the children and parentnode fields, as well as of permuting the data of clt.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"HMatrices.split!"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#LinearAlgebra.cholesky!-Tuple{LinearAlgebra.Hermitian{T, HMatrix{R, T}} where {R, T}, Any}","page":"References","text":"cholesky!(M::HMatrix,comp)\n\nHierarchical Cholesky factorization of M, using comp to generate the compressed blocks during the multiplication routines.\n\nF \\ b solves in the ordering of the points used to assemble M. The factors F.L and F.U are stored in the ordering of the cluster tree, with F.L * (F.U * x) ≈ Matrix(M)[F.p, F.p] * x; products and solves with them act in that ordering.\n\nThe factorization is currently serial: threads is accepted but not used.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.cholesky!"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#LinearAlgebra.cholesky!-Tuple{LinearAlgebra.Hermitian{T, HMatrix{R, T}} where {R, T}}","page":"References","text":"cholesky!(M::HMatrix; atol, rank, rtol, threads = true)\n\nHierarchical Cholesky factorization of M, using the PartialACA(; atol, rank, rtol) compressor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.cholesky!"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#LinearAlgebra.cholesky-Tuple{LinearAlgebra.Hermitian{T, HMatrix{R, T}} where {R, T}, Vararg{Any}}","page":"References","text":"cholesky(M::HMatrix,args...;kwargs...)\n\nHierarchical cholesky factorization. See cholesky! for the available options.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.cholesky"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#LinearAlgebra.lu!-Tuple{HMatrix, Any}","page":"References","text":"lu!(M::HMatrix,comp)\n\nHierarchical LU factorization of M, using comp to generate the compressed blocks during the multiplication routines.\n\nF \\ b solves in the ordering of the points used to assemble M. The factors F.L and F.U are stored in the ordering of the cluster trees, with F.L * (F.U * x) ≈ Matrix(M)[F.p, F.q] * x; products and solves with them act in that ordering.\n\nThe factorization is currently serial: threads is accepted but not used.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.lu!"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#LinearAlgebra.lu!-Tuple{HMatrix}","page":"References","text":"lu!(M::HMatrix; atol, rank, rtol, threads = true)\n\nHierarchical LU factorization of M, using the PartialACA(; atol, rank, rtol) compressor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.lu!"},{"category":"method","location":"references/#LinearAlgebra.lu-Tuple{HMatrix, Vararg{Any}}","page":"References","text":"LinearAlgebra.lu(M::HMatrix,args...;kwargs...)\n\nHierarchical LU factorization. See lu! for the available options.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.lu"},{"category":"function","location":"references/#LinearAlgebra.mul!","page":"References","text":"mul!(y::AbstractVector,H::HMatrix,x::AbstractVector,a,b[;threads])\n\nPerform y <-- H*x*a + y*b in place.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.mul!"},{"category":"section","location":"#home-section","page":"Getting started","text":"A package for assembling and factoring hierarchical matrices","title":"HMatrices.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Overview","page":"Getting started","text":"This package provides some functionality for assembling as well as for doing linear algebra with hierarchical matrices. The main structure exported is the HMatrix type, which can be used to efficiently approximate certain linear operators containing a hierarchical low-rank structure. Once assembled, a hierarchical matrix can be used to accelerate the solution of Ax=b in a variety of ways. Below you will find a quick introduction for how to assemble and utilize an HMatrix; see the References section for more information on the available methods and structures.\n\nnote: Note\nAlthough hierarchical matrices have a broad range of application, this package focuses on their use to approximate integral operators arising in boundary integral equation (BIE) methods. As such, most of the API has been designed with BIEs in mind, and the examples that follow will focus on the compression of integral operators. Feel free to open an issue or reach out if you have an interesting application of hierarchical matrices in mind not covered by this package!\n\ntip: Useful references\nThe notation and algorithms implemented were mostly drawn from the following references:Hackbusch, Wolfgang. Hierarchical matrices: algorithms and analysis. Vol. 49. Heidelberg: Springer, 2015.\nBebendorf, Mario. Hierarchical matrices. Springer Berlin Heidelberg, 2008.","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"#assemble-generic-subsection","page":"Getting started","text":"To assemble an HMatrix you need two ingredients:\n\nthe matrix-like object K that you wish to compress, which only needs to support K[i,j];\npoints associated with the rows and the columns of K, which are used to group the indices into clusters whose interactions can be compressed.\n\nTo illustrate how this is done for a concrete problem, consider two set of points X = left boldsymbolx_i right_i=1^m and Y =leftboldsymbolx_j right_j=1^n in mathbbR^2, and let K be a m times n matrix with entries given by:\n\n  K_ij = G(boldsymbolx_iboldsymboly_j)\n\nfor some kernel function G. To make things simple, we will take X and Y to be points distributed on a circle:\n\nusing HMatrices, LinearAlgebra, StaticArrays\nconst Point2D = SVector{2,Float64}\n\n# points on a circle\nm = n = 10_000\nX = Y = [Point2D(sin(i*2π/n),cos(i*2π/n)) for i in 0:n-1]\nnothing\n\nNext we will create the matrix-like structure to represent the object K. We will pick G to be the free-space Greens function for Laplace's equation in two-dimensions:\n\nstruct LaplaceMatrix <: AbstractMatrix{Float64}\n  X::Vector{Point2D}\n  Y::Vector{Point2D}\nend\n\nBase.getindex(K::LaplaceMatrix,i::Int,j::Int) = -1/2π*log(norm(K.X[i] - K.Y[j]) + 1e-10)\nBase.size(K::LaplaceMatrix) = length(K.X), length(K.Y)\n\n# create the abstract matrix\nK = LaplaceMatrix(X,Y)\n10000×10000 Main.var\"Main\".LaplaceMatrix:\n 3.66468   1.17336   1.06305   0.998514  …  0.998514  1.06305   1.17336\n 1.17336   3.66468   1.17336   1.06305      0.952728  0.998514  1.06305\n 1.06305   1.17336   3.66468   1.17336      0.917214  0.952728  0.998514\n 0.998514  1.06305   1.17336   3.66468      0.888197  0.917214  0.952728\n 0.952728  0.998514  1.06305   1.17336      0.863663  0.888197  0.917214\n 0.917214  0.952728  0.998514  1.06305   …  0.842411  0.863663  0.888197\n 0.888197  0.917214  0.952728  0.998514     0.823665  0.842411  0.863663\n 0.863663  0.888197  0.917214  0.952728     0.806896  0.823665  0.842411\n 0.842411  0.863663  0.888197  0.917214     0.791727  0.806896  0.823665\n 0.823665  0.842411  0.863663  0.888197     0.777879  0.791727  0.806896\n ⋮                                       ⋱                      \n 0.823665  0.806896  0.791727  0.777879     0.888197  0.863663  0.842411\n 0.842411  0.823665  0.806896  0.791727     0.917214  0.888197  0.863663\n 0.863663  0.842411  0.823665  0.806896     0.952728  0.917214  0.888197\n 0.888197  0.863663  0.842411  0.823665     0.998514  0.952728  0.917214\n 0.917214  0.888197  0.863663  0.842411  …  1.06305   0.998514  0.952728\n 0.952728  0.917214  0.888197  0.863663     1.17336   1.06305   0.998514\n 0.998514  0.952728  0.917214  0.888197     3.66468   1.17336   1.06305\n 1.06305   0.998514  0.952728  0.917214     1.17336   3.66468   1.17336\n 1.17336   1.06305   0.998514  0.952728     1.06305   1.17336   3.66468\n\nWe can now assemble an approximation of K, passing the points associated with its rows and columns:\n\nH = assemble_hmatrix(K, X, Y; atol = 1e-6, threads = false)\nHMatrix of Float64 with range 1:10000 × 1:10000\n\t number of nodes in tree: 3353\n\t number of leaves: 2515 (800 admissible + 1715 full)\n\t min rank of sparse blocks : 5\n\t max rank of sparse blocks : 8\n\t min length of dense blocks : 625\n\t max length of dense blocks : 4300\n\t min number of elements per leaf: 625\n\t max number of elements per leaf: 2777222\n\t depth of tree: 9\n\t compression ratio: 21.814323\n\n\nThe keyword arguments of assemble_hmatrix control the accuracy of the low-rank blocks (atol, rtol and rank), which blocks are compressed (eta), and the size of the smallest clusters (nmax). If K is a Hermitian matrix, wrapping it as Hermitian(K) assembles only half of it and allows for a Cholesky factorization. When K is a KernelMatrix, the points can be omitted (see the Kernel matrices section).\n\ntip: Finer control\nThe assembly can also be given its ingredients directly: cluster trees partitioning the rows and the columns, an admissibility condition to decide which blocks are compressed, and a compressor for those blocks. For example,using HMatrices: ClusterTree, CardinalitySplitter, StrongAdmissibilityStd, PartialACA\nXclt = ClusterTree(X, CardinalitySplitter(; nmax = 32))\nH = assemble_hmatrix(K, Xclt, Xclt; adm = StrongAdmissibilityStd(; eta = 2), comp = PartialACA(; atol = 1e-6))\n\nYou can now use H in lieu of K (as an approximation) for certain linear algebra operations, as shown next.","title":"Assembling an HMatrix"},{"category":"section","location":"#Matrix-vector-product-and-iterative-solvers","page":"Getting started","text":"The simplest operation you can perform with an HMatrix is to multiply it by a vector:\n\nx = rand(n)\nnorm(H*x - K*x)\n4.99915875752608e-6\n\nMore advanced options (such as choosing between a threaded or serial implementation) can be accessed by calling mul! directly:\n\ny = similar(x)\nmul!(y,H,x,1,0;threads=false)\nnorm(y - K*x)\n4.99915875752608e-6\n\nThe example below illustrates how to use the HMatrix H constructed above with the IterativeSolvers package:\n\nusing IterativeSolvers\nb = rand(m)\napprox = gmres!(y,H,b;abstol=1e-6)\nexact  = Matrix(K)\\b\nnorm(approx-exact)/norm(exact)\n7.458445046986818e-5\n\nInternally, H is stored in the ordering induced by the clustering of the points, but every operation, such as H*x, mul!, Matrix(H), or the solves with a factorization, works in the ordering of the points given by the user. The cost of permuting the vectors is negligible compared to that of the products.\n\nnote: Problem size\nFor \"small\" problem sizes, the overhead associated with the more complex structure of an HMatrix will lead to computational times that are larger than the dense representation, even when the HMatrix occupies less memory. For large problem sizes, however, the loglinear complexity will yield significant gains in terms of memory and cpu time provided the underlying operator has a hierarchical low-rank structure.","title":"Matrix vector product and iterative solvers"},{"category":"section","location":"#Factorization-and-direct-solvers","page":"Getting started","text":"Although the forward map illustrated in the example above suffices to solve the linear system Kx = b using an iterative solver, there are circumstances where a direct solver is desirable (because, e.g., the system is not well-conditioned or you wish to solve it for many right-hand-sides b). The hierarchical LU factorization of H can be computed as follows (and a hierarchical Cholesky factorization is available for an H assembled from a Hermitian matrix):\n\nF = lu(H;atol=1e-6)\nLU factorization of HMatrix of Float64 with range 1:10000 × 1:10000\n\t number of nodes in tree: 3353\n\t number of leaves: 2515 (800 admissible + 1715 full)\n\t min rank of sparse blocks : 4\n\t max rank of sparse blocks : 8\n\t min length of dense blocks : 625\n\t max length of dense blocks : 4300\n\t min number of elements per leaf: 625\n\t max number of elements per leaf: 2777222\n\t depth of tree: 9\n\t compression ratio: 22.218055\n\n\n\nNote that unliked the matrix-vector product, factoring H is not exact in the sense that lu(H) ≠ lu(Matrix(H)). The accuracy of the approximation can be controlled through the keyword arguments atol, rtol and rank, which are used in the various intermediate truncations performed during the factorization. See lu for more details.\n\nimportant: Truncation error\nThe parameters atol and rtol are used to control the truncation of low-rank blocks adaptively using an estimate of the true error (in Frobenius norm). These local errors may accumulate after successive truncations, meaning that the global approximation error (in Frobenius norm) may be larger than the prescribed tolerance.\n\nThe returned object F is of the LU type, and efficient routines are provided to solve linear system using F:\n\napprox = F\\b\nnorm(approx-exact)/norm(exact)\n2.637010693939181e-8\n\nLike H, F \\ b works in the ordering of the points. The factors F.L and F.U are stored in the ordering of the clusters, and F.L * (F.U * x) ≈ Matrix(H)[F.p, F.q] * x, as for the pivoted factorizations of LinearAlgebra.\n\nNote that the error in solving the linear system may be significantly larger than the error in computing H*x due to the condition of the underlying operator.\n\ntip: Tip\nBecause factoring an HMatrix with a small error tolerance can be quite time-consuming, a hybrid strategy commonly employed consists of using a rough factorization (with e.g. large tolerance or a fixed rank) as a preconditioner to an iterative solver.","title":"Factorization and direct solvers"},{"category":"section","location":"#Other-kernels","page":"Getting started","text":"So far we focused on the (manual) compression of a simple kernel matrix where the entry (i,j) depended only on a function G and on point-clouds X and Y. There are many interesting applications where computing the (i,j) entry requires more information, such as triangles, basis functions, or normal vectors. To illustrate how the methods discussed before could be adapted lets construct a double-layer matrix for Laplace equation. To keep the example simple, we will re-use the point clouds X and Y defined before, and we will simply append the normal vector information to a LaplaceDoubleLayer structure. The implementation could look something like this:\n\nstruct LaplaceDoubleLayer <: AbstractMatrix{Float64}\n    X::Vector{Point2D}\n    Y::Vector{Point2D}\n    NY::Vector{Point2D} # normals at Y coordinate\nend\n\nfunction Base.getindex(K::LaplaceDoubleLayer,i::Int,j::Int)\n    r = K.X[i] - K.Y[j]\n    d = norm(r) + 1e-10\n    return (1 / (2π) / (d^2) * dot(r, K.NY[j]))\nend\nBase.size(K::LaplaceDoubleLayer) = length(K.X), length(K.Y)\n\nWe can now simply instantiate a double-layer kernel, and compress it as before\n\n# create the abstract matrix\nny = Y\nK = LaplaceDoubleLayer(X,Y,ny)\nH = assemble_hmatrix(K, X, Y; atol = 1e-6, threads = false)\nHMatrix of Float64 with range 1:10000 × 1:10000\n\t number of nodes in tree: 3353\n\t number of leaves: 2515 (800 admissible + 1715 full)\n\t min rank of sparse blocks : 2\n\t max rank of sparse blocks : 2\n\t min length of dense blocks : 625\n\t max length of dense blocks : 4300\n\t min number of elements per leaf: 625\n\t max number of elements per leaf: 2777222\n\t depth of tree: 9\n\t compression ratio: 31.424198\n\n\nWith H assembled, everything else works exactly as before!","title":"Other kernels"},{"category":"section","location":"#Index","page":"Getting started","text":"","title":"Index"}]
}
